# Map Guide 2 · Part B World, chapters 7 to 10.
P = lambda n, g, x, **kw: dict(n=n, g=g, x=x, **kw)

CH_WORLD = [
dict(id="straits", part="World", t="Straits, canals and capes", intro=(
 "A strait is a narrow stretch of sea between two pieces of land that joins two larger seas. A canal is a waterway people dug for the same job. A cape is a point of land sticking out into the sea. "
 "Straits and canals are choke points: block one, and ships must go thousands of kilometres round, so prices of oil and goods jump. That is why they keep coming into the news. "
 "Learn each with three things: the two lands on its sides, the two waters it joins, and why it is in the news."), P=[
 P("Strait of Hormuz", [56.40, 26.50], "A strait about 33 km wide at its narrowest, between Iran (north) and the Musandam tip of Oman (south). It is the only sea way out of the Persian Gulf into the Gulf of Oman and the Arabian Sea. About a fifth of the world's oil and much of its liquefied gas pass through it, including a large share of India's oil imports from Iraq, Saudi Arabia, the UAE and Kuwait. Iran has often threatened to close it, most recently during the Israel–Iran war of June 2025."),
 P("Bab-el-Mandeb", [43.40, 12.60], "Arabic for 'gate of tears', a strait about 30 km wide between Yemen (Arabia) and Djibouti and Eritrea (Africa). It joins the Red Sea to the Gulf of Aden and the Arabian Sea, so every ship using the Suez Canal passes through it. From late 2023 the Houthis, a Yemeni armed group, attacked ships here; many shipping lines went round the Cape of Good Hope instead, and the Indian Navy deployed warships to protect trade. Djibouti hosts military bases of the USA, China, France and Japan."),
 P("Suez Canal", [32.35, 30.60], "A sea-level canal about 193 km long across Egypt, joining Port Said on the Mediterranean to Suez on the Red Sea. It was built by a French company under Ferdinand de Lesseps and opened in 1869. It cut the sea route from Europe to India by about 7,000 km, since ships no longer had to sail round Africa. Egypt's Nasser nationalised it in 1956, which led to the Suez Crisis. In March 2021 the giant ship Ever Given got stuck in it for six days and blocked world trade."),
 P("Strait of Malacca", [100.50, 3.00], "A long, narrow strait (about 800 km) between the Malay Peninsula (Malaysia) and the island of Sumatra (Indonesia), with Singapore at its southern end. It joins the Andaman Sea (Indian Ocean) to the South China Sea (Pacific). It is the shortest sea route between India and China, and most of China's oil passes through it; China's worry about this is called the 'Malacca dilemma'. India's Andaman and Nicobar Islands sit at its western entrance."),
 P("Strait of Gibraltar", [-5.60, 35.97], "A strait only about 14 km wide between Spain (Europe) and Morocco (Africa), joining the Mediterranean Sea to the Atlantic Ocean. The Rock of Gibraltar, a British territory, stands on its north side; the Spanish town of Ceuta lies on the African side. In old times it was called the Pillars of Hercules, the end of the known world."),
 P("Bosporus", [29.05, 41.12], "A narrow strait (about 30 km long) running through Istanbul, Turkey, joining the Black Sea to the Sea of Marmara. With the Dardanelles further south, it is the only sea way from the Black Sea to the Mediterranean, so Russia's and Ukraine's grain and oil ships must pass Turkey. It divides Europe from Asia. The Montreux Convention (1936) lets Turkey control warships passing through."),
 P("Kerch Strait", [36.50, 45.30], "A strait between the Crimean peninsula (west) and Russia's Taman peninsula (east), joining the Black Sea to the shallow Sea of Azov. Russia built the 19 km Crimean Bridge over it after taking Crimea from Ukraine in 2014; the bridge has been attacked during the war. Ukraine's port of Mariupol is on the Sea of Azov. The 2021 paper asked about Ukraine's coastline along the Sea of Azov.", y="2021 Q17"),
 P("Strait of Dover", [1.45, 51.00], "The narrowest part of the English Channel, about 33 km wide, between Dover in England and Calais in France. It joins the English Channel to the North Sea and is one of the busiest shipping lanes in the world. The Channel Tunnel ('Chunnel', 1994), a rail tunnel of about 50 km, runs under it."),
 P("Panama Canal", [-79.68, 9.08], "A canal about 82 km long across the Isthmus of Panama (an isthmus is a narrow strip of land joining two bigger lands), joining the Atlantic (Caribbean Sea) to the Pacific. It opened in 1914; the USA built and ran it until handing it to Panama in 1999. Because the land is high, ships are lifted up and down by locks (water steps) using water from Gatun Lake. Droughts in 2023–24 lowered the lake and cut the number of ships."),
 P("Bering Strait", [-169.00, 65.80], "A strait about 82 km wide between Russia's Chukotka (Asia) and Alaska (North America, USA), joining the Pacific (Bering Sea) to the Arctic Ocean. The International Date Line runs through it, so the two Diomede islands in the middle are a day apart. In the Ice Age it was dry land, the land bridge over which the first people walked into the Americas."),
 P("Taiwan Strait", [119.50, 24.50], "A strait about 180 km wide between mainland China (Fujian) and the island of Taiwan, joining the East China Sea to the South China Sea. China claims Taiwan as its own; China's frequent military drills around the island make the strait one of the world's most watched hot spots. Taiwan makes most of the world's advanced computer chips."),
 P("Mozambique Channel", [41.00, -18.00], "A wide arm of the Indian Ocean, about 1,600 km long, between Mozambique on the African mainland and the island of Madagascar. Ships going round the Cape of Good Hope often use it. India helps patrol it and has a radar and facilities on Mauritius' Agaléga islands to the east."),
 P("Cape of Good Hope", [18.47, -34.36], "A rocky headland near Cape Town, South Africa. The Portuguese Bartolomeu Dias first sailed round it in 1488, and Vasco da Gama rounded it in 1497 on his way to Calicut (Kozhikode), which he reached in May 1498, opening the sea route from Europe to India. It is not the southern tip of Africa; that is Cape Agulhas, where the Atlantic and Indian Oceans meet. When the Red Sea is unsafe, ships go round it."),
]),
dict(id="worldrelief", part="World", t="Mountains, volcanoes and trenches", intro=(
 "The earth's outer shell is broken into large plates that move a few centimetres a year. Where plates push together (converge), rock folds up into fold mountains, or one plate dives under another (subduction), making deep trenches and chains of volcanoes. Where plates pull apart (diverge), magma rises to fill the gap, making ridges and rift valleys. "
 "Young fold mountains are high and sharp (Himalaya, Alps, Andes, Rockies); old ones are low and worn (Appalachians, Urals, Aravalli). A block mountain (horst) is a block lifted between two faults (cracks), and the land that sinks beside it is a rift valley (graben). A relict mountain is the worn stump of an old range. "
 "About three-quarters of the world's active volcanoes lie round the Pacific Ocean, on the Ring of Fire. A few stand in the middle of a plate over a hot spot, a plume of hot rock rising from deep inside the earth."), P=[
 P("Mount Everest", [86.93, 27.99], "At 8,849 m (measured by Nepal and China in 2020), the highest peak on earth, on the border of Nepal and Tibet. Nepalis call it Sagarmatha and Tibetans Chomolungma. It is named after George Everest, a Surveyor General of India. Tenzing Norgay and Edmund Hillary first climbed it on 29 May 1953. Bachendri Pal (1984) was the first Indian woman to reach the top."),
 P("Alps", [10.00, 46.40], "Young fold mountains of Europe, an arc about 1,200 km long across France, Italy, Switzerland, Austria and nearby countries, formed as the African plate pushed into Europe. Mont Blanc (about 4,800 m) on the France–Italy border is the highest. Rivers such as the Rhine, Rhône and Po rise here. Glaciers carved deep valleys and lakes such as Geneva and Como."),
 P("Andes", [-70.00, -20.00], "The longest mountain range on land, about 7,000 km down the whole west side of South America. They are young fold mountains with many volcanoes, made where the oceanic Nazca plate dives under the South American plate; so these two plates converge. Aconcagua in Argentina (about 6,960 m) is the highest peak outside Asia. Lake Titicaca and the Inca city of Machu Picchu are in the Andes.", y="2021 Q61"),
 P("Rockies", [-110.00, 45.00], "Young fold mountains running about 4,800 km from Canada to New Mexico in western North America. They form the Continental Divide: rivers to their west flow to the Pacific, rivers to their east to the Atlantic or Arctic. The warm Chinook wind blows down their eastern slopes. Yellowstone, the first national park in the world (1872), sits on a hot spot here."),
 P("Appalachians", [-80.00, 38.00], "Old fold mountains running about 2,400 km along the eastern USA into Canada. They were once as high as the Himalaya, but about 300 million years of rain and ice have worn them into low, rounded ridges (the highest, Mount Mitchell, is about 2,000 m). They are rich in coal. The 2021 paper matched them with 'old folded mountain'.", y="2021 Q64"),
 P("Ural Mountains", [59.50, 58.00], "Old fold mountains running about 2,500 km north to south through Russia, from the Arctic coast to Kazakhstan, formed about 300 million years ago. With the Ural river and the Caspian, they are taken as the boundary between Europe and Asia. They are low (the highest peak is under 1,900 m) and worn, and very rich in minerals: iron, copper, gold, platinum and gems. In the Second World War the Soviet Union moved its factories here, out of German reach."),
 P("Black Forest", [8.20, 48.20], "A wooded upland in south-west Germany and the textbook example of a block mountain (horst). East of it the land rose; between it and the Vosges mountains of France the land sank, making the Rhine rift valley (graben) in which the Rhine flows north. The Danube rises in the Black Forest. Cuckoo clocks and the Black Forest cake come from here.", y="2021 Q64"),
 P("Scottish Highlands", [-4.50, 57.00], "The mountainous north of Scotland, the worn stumps of the very old Caledonian mountains, so they are called relict (left-over) mountains. Ben Nevis (1,345 m) is the highest peak in the British Isles. Ice Age glaciers carved long valleys filled with lakes ('lochs'), such as Loch Ness.", y="2021 Q64"),
 P("Aleutian Islands", [-176.00, 52.00], "A chain of about 70 volcanic islands curving about 1,900 km west from Alaska across the North Pacific towards Russia's Kamchatka. They formed where the Pacific plate dives under the North American plate, making the deep Aleutian Trench beside them. They have one of the highest numbers of active volcanoes of any region on earth, part of the Ring of Fire. Japan occupied two of them in the Second World War.", y="2021 Q65"),
 P("Iceland", [-19.00, 64.90], "An island country in the North Atlantic that sits right on the Mid-Atlantic Ridge, where the North American and Eurasian plates move apart (diverge); so the island is slowly being torn in two. It has many volcanoes (Eyjafjallajökull's ash cloud stopped European flights in 2010; the Reykjanes peninsula has erupted often since 2021), hot springs and geysers (the word 'geyser' comes from Geysir in Iceland). Almost all its electricity comes from geothermal heat and water power.", y="2021 Q61"),
 P("Hawaii", [-155.50, 19.60], "A chain of volcanic islands in the middle of the Pacific plate, a US state. They were made by a hot spot: as the plate slides over a fixed plume of hot rock, a line of volcanoes forms, oldest in the north-west and youngest (the Big Island) in the south-east. Mauna Loa and Kilauea are among the most active volcanoes on earth; measured from the sea floor, Mauna Kea is taller than Everest."),
 P("Mount Fuji", [138.73, 35.36], "Japan's highest mountain (3,776 m) and a near-perfect cone, a stratovolcano (built of layers of ash and lava) on the island of Honshu, about 100 km west of Tokyo. It last erupted in 1707 and is classed as active. It is a sacred symbol of Japan and a World Heritage Site. Japan lies on the Ring of Fire, where four plates meet, so it has many quakes and volcanoes."),
 P("Kilimanjaro", [37.35, -3.07], "The highest mountain in Africa (5,895 m), a dormant volcano in north-east Tanzania near the Kenyan border, close to the equator yet capped with snow and glaciers, which are shrinking fast. Its highest point is Uhuru ('freedom') Peak. It is the tallest free-standing mountain in the world (not part of a range)."),
 P("Great Rift Valley", [36.00, -1.00], "A long system of rift valleys from the Red Sea through Ethiopia, Kenya and Tanzania down to Mozambique, where the African plate is slowly splitting into two (the Nubian and Somali plates). The land sank between faults, and long, deep lakes formed: Tanganyika (the second deepest lake in the world), Malawi, Turkana. Some of the oldest human fossils, such as 'Lucy' in Ethiopia, were found here."),
 P("Mariana Trench", [142.20, 11.35], "A crescent-shaped trench in the western Pacific east of the Mariana Islands (Guam). It formed where the Pacific plate dives under the small Mariana plate. Its deepest point, the Challenger Deep, is about 10,935 m below the sea surface, the deepest known point of the oceans; Everest would fit inside it with room to spare. India's Samudrayaan mission plans to send people 6,000 m down in the Indian Ocean in the submersible Matsya-6000."),
 P("Dead Sea", [35.50, 31.50], "A salt lake between Jordan (east) and Israel and the West Bank (west), in a rift valley. Its shore, about 430 m below sea level, is the lowest land on earth. Its water is about ten times saltier than the ocean, so people float easily and almost nothing lives in it. The Jordan river flows in and nothing flows out; water leaves only by evaporation, and the lake is shrinking."),
]),
dict(id="lands", part="World", t="Grasslands and deserts", intro=(
 "Grasslands grow where there is too little rain for forest but too much for desert. Temperate grasslands lie in the middle of continents in cooler latitudes, and they have a different name on each continent; the 2021 paper set a trap with them. Learn the set together: Prairies in North America, Pampas in South America, Veldt in South Africa, Steppes in Eurasia, Downs in Australia, Canterbury in New Zealand. "
 "Tropical grasslands, near the equator with a long dry season, are called savannas: Llanos (Venezuela) and Campos (Brazil) in South America, and the Serengeti in East Africa. "
 "Deserts form where air sinks and stays dry (around 20–30° north and south of the equator), deep inside continents far from the sea, behind mountains (in a rain shadow), or along coasts washed by cold ocean currents."), P=[
 P("Prairies", [-100.00, 45.00], "The temperate grasslands of the central USA and Canada, between the Rockies and the Mississippi. Their deep black soil once fed huge herds of bison; today they are the wheat and maize belt of North America, called the 'bread basket of the world'. Their winters are bitterly cold and summers hot.", y="2021 Q63"),
 P("Pampas", [-62.00, -35.00], "The flat temperate grasslands of Argentina, Uruguay and southern Brazil, around Buenos Aires. They are rich farmland for wheat and for cattle ranching, worked by cowboys called gauchos; Argentina's beef comes from here. The 2021 trap put the Pampas in North America.", y="2021 Q63"),
 P("Veldt", [27.00, -27.00], "The temperate grasslands of the high plateau of South Africa (and parts of Zimbabwe and Botswana). The word is Afrikaans (Dutch) for 'field'. Being high above sea level, it is mild. It is used for maize and sheep and cattle farming; gold and diamonds are mined nearby (Johannesburg grew on the Witwatersrand gold field).", y="2021 Q63", c="South Africa"),
 P("Steppes", [60.00, 48.00], "The vast temperate grasslands of Eurasia, from Ukraine and southern Russia through Kazakhstan to Mongolia. Nomadic horsemen such as the Scythians and the Mongols of Genghis Khan rode across them. Ukraine's black soil (chernozem) steppe made it the 'bread basket of Europe'.", y="2021 Q63"),
 P("Downs", [145.00, -27.00], "The temperate grasslands of the Murray–Darling basin in south-eastern Australia, west of the Great Dividing Range, which keeps rain off them. They are known for sheep rearing, especially merino sheep, whose fine wool makes Australia the world's largest wool producer, and for wheat. Water for animals comes from artesian wells, where underground water rises by its own pressure, from the Great Artesian Basin."),
 P("Llanos", [-68.00, 7.00], "Tropical grasslands (savanna) of Venezuela and Colombia, on the wide plains of the Orinoco river and its tributaries. In the wet season (May to October) the rivers flood large areas; in the dry season the grass turns brown and many pools dry up. Cattle ranching by cowboys called llaneros is the main work. Capybaras (the largest rodents) and the Orinoco crocodile live here."),
 P("Campos", [-48.00, -15.00], "Tropical grasslands of the Brazilian Highlands, south of the Amazon forest. The wider savanna of central Brazil is called the Cerrado, the most species-rich savanna in the world. Much of it has been cleared for soybean farms and cattle, which has made Brazil the world's largest soybean grower and exporter; this clearing gets less attention than the Amazon's. Brasília, the planned capital, was built on it."),
 P("Serengeti", [34.80, -2.30], "A savanna in northern Tanzania, joined across the border to Kenya's Maasai Mara. Every year more than a million wildebeest, with zebras and gazelles, walk in a great circle following the rains: the Great Migration. The Serengeti National Park is a World Heritage Site. The Maasai people herd cattle here."),
 P("Sahara", [10.00, 23.00], "The largest hot desert in the world (about 9 million sq km, nearly the size of the USA), across North Africa from the Atlantic to the Red Sea. Only about a quarter of it is sand dunes (ergs); most is stone and gravel plain. The Nile is the only river to cross it. Its dust, blown across the Atlantic, fertilises the Amazon. The Sahel is the dry belt along its southern edge."),
 P("Kalahari", [22.00, -23.00], "A semi-desert covering most of Botswana and parts of Namibia and South Africa. It gets some rain, so it has grass and thorn bush. The San (Bushmen), some of the oldest hunter-gatherer peoples, live here. The Okavango river ends in a huge inland delta on its edge instead of reaching the sea."),
 P("Atacama", [-69.50, -24.00], "A coastal desert in northern Chile, the driest non-polar desert on earth; some weather stations have never recorded rain. It is so dry because the cold Humboldt current offshore cools the air, and the Andes block moist air from the east. It is rich in copper, nitrate and lithium, and its clear skies host big telescopes."),
 P("Gobi", [105.00, 43.00], "A cold desert in Mongolia and northern China. It is dry because it lies far inside Asia and in the rain shadow of the Himalaya and Tibetan Plateau. Winters are very cold. Its sand storms blow across to Beijing and Korea. Many dinosaur fossils and the first known dinosaur eggs were found here."),
 P("Rub' al Khali", [50.00, 20.00], "The 'Empty Quarter', the largest continuous sand desert in the world, covering much of southern Saudi Arabia and parts of Oman, the UAE and Yemen. Its dunes can be over 250 m high. Huge oil fields, such as Ghawar, lie under the deserts of eastern Arabia."),
]),
dict(id="worldrivers", part="World", t="World rivers, deltas and lakes", intro=(
 "A delta is new land a river builds at its mouth from the silt it carries; it forms where the river drops silt faster than waves and tides can wash it away. Its shape tells you how calm the sea is: a fan-shaped (arcuate) delta like the Nile's, long fingers like the Mississippi's bird-foot, or a pointed (cuspate) one. "
 "Where tides are strong, the river ends in an open, funnel-shaped mouth called an estuary, with no delta, like the Amazon, Thames or Narmada. "
 "For lakes, the exam asks superlatives again: largest, deepest, highest. Be careful: the Caspian is the largest lake of all but salty, Superior is the largest freshwater lake by area, and Baikal holds the most fresh water."), P=[
 P("Mississippi delta", [-89.30, 29.10], "The Mississippi (with its main tributary the Missouri, one of the longest river systems in the world) drains most of the central USA and ends in the calm Gulf of Mexico near New Orleans. Its channels push long fingers of silt far into the sea, making the classic bird-foot delta, shaped like a bird's toes. The delta is sinking and losing land fast; Hurricane Katrina flooded New Orleans in 2005.", y="2021 Q66", r="Mississippi"),
 P("Nile delta", [31.00, 31.00], "The Nile (about 6,650 km) is usually taken as the longest river in the world. Its two branches, the White Nile (from Lake Victoria) and the Blue Nile (from Lake Tana in Ethiopia), meet at Khartoum, Sudan. It ends in the Mediterranean in a fan-shaped (arcuate) delta, the classic arcuate delta, where most Egyptians live. The Aswan High Dam (1970) stopped its yearly floods. Ethiopia's new Grand Ethiopian Renaissance Dam on the Blue Nile worries Egypt.", r="Nile"),
 P("Lake Victoria", [33.00, -1.00], "The largest lake in Africa and the largest tropical lake in the world, shared by Uganda, Kenya and Tanzania. It is the main source of the White Nile, which leaves it at Jinja, Uganda. It was named by the British explorer John Speke after Queen Victoria. It is shallow, and the invasive water hyacinth and Nile perch have harmed it."),
 P("Amazon mouth", [-50.00, 0.00], "The Amazon carries more water than any other river (about a fifth of all the river water flowing into the oceans) and has the largest drainage basin, mostly in Brazil. It rises in the Andes of Peru and meets the Atlantic near the equator. Its mouth is a huge estuary with islands such as Marajó, not a proper delta, because strong tides and currents carry its silt away. Its rainforest is the largest on earth, sometimes called the lungs of the planet.", r="Amazonas"),
 P("Niger delta", [6.00, 4.80], "The Niger (about 4,180 km, the main river of West Africa) flows in a big boomerang shape from Guinea through Mali, Niger and Nigeria. It ends in a large arcuate delta in Nigeria on the Gulf of Guinea, full of mangroves. The delta holds most of Nigeria's oil, and oil spills have badly polluted it."),
 P("Congo river", [12.40, -6.00], "The Congo (about 4,700 km) is the deepest river in the world and second only to the Amazon in the water it carries. It is the only major river that crosses the equator twice, so some part of its basin is always in the rainy season and its flow stays steady. It flows through the second largest rainforest on earth. Its mouth is an estuary on the Atlantic between the two Congos and Angola."),
 P("Danube", [29.50, 45.10], "The Danube (about 2,850 km), Europe's second longest river after the Volga, rises in the Black Forest of Germany and flows east to the Black Sea, where it builds a delta in Romania and Ukraine. It flows through or along ten countries, more than any other river in the world, and through four capitals: Vienna, Bratislava, Budapest and Belgrade.", r="Danube"),
 P("Caspian Sea", [50.50, 41.50], "The largest lake in the world by area (about 371,000 sq km), between Europe and Asia. It is called a sea because its water is salty (about a third as salty as the ocean). Five countries touch it: Russia, Kazakhstan, Turkmenistan, Iran and Azerbaijan. The Volga flows in; nothing flows out. It has big oil and gas fields (Baku) and the sturgeon that gives caviar."),
 P("Lake Baikal", [108.00, 53.50], "In Siberia, Russia, the deepest lake in the world (about 1,640 m) and the oldest (about 25 million years). It holds about a fifth of all the fresh unfrozen surface water on earth, more than all the North American Great Lakes together. It lies in a rift valley. It has its own freshwater seal, the nerpa. A World Heritage Site."),
 P("Lake Superior", [-87.50, 47.70], "The largest of North America's five Great Lakes (Superior, Michigan, Huron, Erie, Ontario) and the largest freshwater lake in the world by area, on the USA–Canada border. The lakes drain through the St Lawrence to the Atlantic; Niagara Falls is between Lakes Erie and Ontario. The Soo Locks let ships go from Superior to Huron."),
 P("Lake Titicaca", [-69.30, -15.80], "A lake on the border of Peru and Bolivia, at about 3,810 m in the Andes. It is called the highest navigable lake in the world (big boats sail on it) and is the largest lake in South America by volume. The Uros people live on floating islands made of reeds. Inca legend says the first Inca rose from it."),
 P("Yangtze mouth", [121.80, 31.40], "The Yangtze (Chang Jiang, about 6,300 km) is the longest river in Asia and the third longest in the world, flowing wholly inside China from Tibet to the East China Sea at Shanghai. The Three Gorges Dam on it is the largest hydropower plant in the world. China's other great river, the Huang He (Yellow River), is called 'China's sorrow' for its floods.", r="Yangtze"),
]),
]
